---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, "/");
---
<Base
  title="mhs — On the fault"
  description="On-fault Coulomb excess, stress changes and slip on three rough faults that break the free surface, one of them a splay rooted on another, one value per element, with a mollified kernel, with a classical triangular dislocation element, and with the mollified kernel carrying slip that is continuous across the elements."
  current="onfault"
>
  <Fragment slot="sidebar">
    <h1>On the fault<span class="sub">one fault alone, then three</span></h1>
    <p>
      Two experiments on one mesh. First a single rough thrust fault, A,
      breaking the free surface, loaded until most of it slips. Then the same
      A with two neighbours added: B, a parallel ramp in its hanging wall, and
      C, a steeper splay rooted on A. A&rsquo;s mesh, its roughness and the
      load are identical in both, so the difference between the two maps is
      exactly what the neighbours do to it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each element is painted with one value, the one read at its centroid,
      where the frictional condition was enforced. Three kernels give the same
      solve: the mollified kernel of this package with one slip per element,
      the classical triangular dislocation element, and the mollified kernel
      with slip continuous across the elements. With the free surface folded
      into the kernel (below) they now agree on the row of elements that
      touch the surface as well.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      Colour is <b>data</b>: one value per element, nothing interpolated, no
      light. The grey sheet is the free surface; the magenta loops are the
      rims, each trace and the junction chord included. In the
      <i>A&nbsp;alone</i> experiment B and C are drawn grey: they are not
      there.
    </div>
    <p class="contact">
      The <i>system &minus; alone</i> view paints the change on A at a window
      of its own, the 99th percentile of the change (a few MPa); widen it to
      the maps&rsquo; window and nothing shows, which is the measurement. The
      legend states the saturated area for every kernel at once.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <div id="viewer" class="onfault">
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="experiment"><span>experiment</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="kernel"><span>kernel</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="metric"><span>metric</span></label>
      <div class="rng" data-ctl="window"><span class="lbl">window</span></div>
    </div>
    <div data-canvas class="canvas" aria-label="Three rough faults breaking the free surface, one a splay rooted on another, coloured by on-fault Coulomb excess, one value per element, for the mollified and the classical kernel"></div>
    <p data-legend class="legend"></p>
    <div class="panel">
      <label data-ctl="mark"><span>mark saturated</span></label>
      <label data-ctl="wire"><span>element edges</span></label>
      <span class="lbl">view</span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="view"></span>
      <span class="btns" data-ctl="shot"></span>
      <label data-ctl="publication"><span>publication view</span></label>
    </div>
    <p data-status class="status">loading&hellip;</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The experiment</h2>
  <p>
    Start with A by itself: a disc of radius 6&nbsp;km dipping 30&deg;, its
    top cut off by the free surface so that it breaks the ground along a
    straight 8.9&nbsp;km trace, with the published rough surface
    (rms facet slope 0.25, correlation length 4&nbsp;km) and 3770 elements of
    side <i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.25&nbsp;km. It carries a uniform friction of
    0.6 on an effective normal stress of 150&nbsp;MPa and is loaded by a
    tectonic shear that is optimal for a 30&deg; thrust, raised to
    &lambda;&nbsp;=&nbsp;1.10&nbsp;&lambda;<sub>0</sub>&nbsp;=&nbsp;129&nbsp;MPa,
    just past the system&rsquo;s first failure. Solved alone, A slips over
    82&nbsp;% of its area with a peak of
    0.82&nbsp;m (mollified kernel); the maps show where it is
    locked and by how much (the Coulomb excess, blue), and the normal and
    shear stress its own slip induces on its own rough surface.
  </p>
  <p>
    Now add B, a disc of radius 4&nbsp;km with the same dip whose trace lies
    6&nbsp;km in front of A&rsquo;s, so that it sits 3&nbsp;km above
    A&rsquo;s plane in the hanging wall where A&rsquo;s slip relaxes the
    load; and C, a 4&nbsp;km disc dipping 50&deg; whose lower edge is the
    line where its plane meets A&rsquo;s, 4.5&nbsp;km deep, so that it is a
    splay growing out of A. A is bit for bit the same mesh, the load is the
    same, and the three faults are solved together. What B and C do to A is
    then a subtraction, the <i>system&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;alone</i> view, and
    what A does to them is read off B and C, which exist only in the second
    experiment. Per kernel:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>kernel</th><th>A active, alone &rarr; system</th>
            <th>A&rsquo;s moment, system / alone</th>
            <th>Coulomb load on A from B and C</th>
            <th>A max slip, alone &rarr; system</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>mollified P0</td><td>0.82 &rarr; 0.82</td><td>&times;0.976</td><td>&minus;0.11 MPa</td><td>0.82 &rarr; 0.82 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td>classical TDE</td><td>0.83 &rarr; 0.82</td><td>&times;0.976</td><td>&minus;0.10 MPa</td><td>0.81 &rarr; 0.81 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td>continuous P2</td><td>0.84 &rarr; 0.83</td><td>&times;0.975</td><td>&minus;0.11 MPa</td><td>0.88 &rarr; 0.88 m</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The neighbours take 2.4&nbsp;% of A&rsquo;s moment and a tenth of a megapascal of Coulomb load from it, and leave its active area and its peak slip where they were: B sits in A&rsquo;s hanging wall where A&rsquo;s own slip has already relaxed the load, and C is a misoriented splay that barely moves. All three kernels say so to the second decimal, which is the first thing the two experiments establish &mdash; the interaction is a property of the geometry and the load, not of the kernel. Read the other way round, A does more to its neighbours than they do to it:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>fault</th><th>active alone</th><th>active in the system</th>
            <th>moment, system / alone</th><th>Coulomb load from the others</th><th>max slip</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>0.82</td><td>0.82</td><td>&times;0.976</td><td>&minus;0.11 MPa</td><td>0.82 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>0.83</td><td>0.83</td><td>&times;0.967</td><td>&minus;0.12 MPa</td><td>1.20 m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>0.11</td><td>0.11</td><td>&times;0.883</td><td><b>&minus;1.25</b> MPa</td><td>0.21 m</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    B, 3&nbsp;km away in A&rsquo;s hanging wall, loses 3&nbsp;% of its moment to A&rsquo;s shadow. C, rooted on A, loses 12&nbsp;%: A&rsquo;s slip unloads the splay above it by 1.25&nbsp;MPa, which on a fault 32&nbsp;MPa below strength keeps it locked everywhere but its deep end. The classical and the continuous kernels give the same fractions, ratios and shadows to the second decimal (the manifest carries all three).
  </p>

  <h2>The system</h2>
  <p>
    Every fault strikes along <i>y</i> and dips toward +<i>x</i>; its trace
    is where the plane meets <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, and the disc&rsquo;s
    centre sits 4, 3 and 2.9&nbsp;km down-dip of it, so each circle is cut by
    the surface and the trace is a straight chord whose vertices lie exactly
    on <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0. C is cut twice: by the surface, and by the
    line where its plane meets A&rsquo;s, 7.8&nbsp;km from A&rsquo;s trace,
    4.5&nbsp;km deep, 5.5&nbsp;km long, at 20&deg; to A. That chord is
    C&rsquo;s lower boundary and an interior line of A&rsquo;s mesh at once:
    its 23 vertices are computed once and written into both meshes, so the
    two tilings share them bit for bit.
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>fault</th><th>radius</th><th>dip</th><th>trace</th><th>elements</th><th>role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>A</b></td><td>6 km</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>8.9 km</td><td>3770</td>
            <td>the main thrust, present in both experiments</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>B</b></td><td>4 km</td><td>30&deg;</td><td>5.3 km</td><td>1730</td>
            <td>a parallel ramp 3 km into A&rsquo;s hanging wall, where A&rsquo;s slip shadows it</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>C</b></td><td>4 km</td><td>50&deg;</td><td>5.5 km</td><td>1558</td>
            <td>a splay rooted on A at 4.5 km depth, misoriented for this load</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    7058 elements in the system. The outline of each fault is sampled on its
    circle and its chords at a spacing of <i>h</i>, and the interior is a
    constrained Delaunay triangulation (Shewchuk&rsquo;s Triangle) with no
    point added on any segment, so every rim vertex lies on its curve and the
    median edge is 1.00&nbsp;<i>h</i> on all three faults. The roughness is
    displaced along each fault&rsquo;s normal and tapered to zero within
    1.5&nbsp;km of a rim or a trace and within 3&nbsp;km of the junction, so
    a rim vertex stays on its curve, a trace vertex on the surface and a
    junction vertex shared. A and B keep the published tilt (rms 14.0&deg;
    and 14.9&deg;); C, 5.8&nbsp;km wide between its two chords, is tapered
    over most of its width and tilts 8.9&deg;. Off the junction, C comes
    within 53&nbsp;m of A, two <i>eps</i>; B&rsquo;s plane is 3&nbsp;km from
    A&rsquo;s. The mollified kernels run at
    <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;25&nbsp;m, and the
    continuous one at 0.2&nbsp;<i>h</i> (why, below).
  </p>

  <h2>The solve</h2>
  <p>
    The condition is Coulomb. At the load &lambda; the solve finds the slip
    &delta; on every element such that each is either locked and below
    strength, or slipping and exactly at it:
    <i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&mu;</i>(<i>&sigma;</i><sub>eff,0</sub>&nbsp;+&nbsp;&lambda;<i>&sigma;<sub>n</sub></i><sup>load</sup>)&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;(&lambda;|<i>&tau;</i><sup>load</sup>|&nbsp;+&nbsp;<i>P</i><sup>T</sup>&delta;)&nbsp;&ge;&nbsp;0,
    with &delta;&nbsp;&middot;&nbsp;<i>w</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0, a linear
    complementarity problem. The slip-induced normal stress is read out but
    not fed back into the strength: with the splay rooted on A that coupling
    costs the operator its positive definiteness (the smallest eigenvalue of sym(&minus;<i>P</i><sub>eff</sub>) is &minus;1.7 for the mollified kernels and &minus;2.7 for the classical one), so the
    solve is the uncoupled problem for every kernel, and whatever the Coulomb
    excess means, it means the same thing in every map.
  </p>
  <p>
    The load is set by a rule, not by eye: the smallest multiple of the
    system&rsquo;s first failure at which A slips over at least 70&nbsp;% of
    its area and B and C are each partly locked and partly slipping. Walked on
    the mollified operator of the three-fault system and then used unchanged
    for every kernel and for A alone:
  </p>
  <div class="wide">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>&lambda; / &lambda;<sub>0</sub></th><th>A active</th><th>B active</th><th>C active</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
                <tr><td>1.05</td><td>0.50</td><td>0.48</td><td>0.06</td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>1.10</b></td><td><b>0.82</b></td><td><b>0.83</b></td><td><b>0.11</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.20</td><td>0.98</td><td>0.93</td><td>0.17</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.35</td><td>1.00</td><td>0.98</td><td>0.22</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1.50</td><td>1.00</td><td>1.00</td><td>0.26</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    At that load the remote shear on A is 105&nbsp;MPa against a
    strength of 106; on C it is 123 against
    155, which is what <i>misoriented</i> means for a 50&deg; plane
    under a load that is optimal at 30&deg;. Solving A alone uses the same
    matrix, restricted to A&rsquo;s rows and columns, and the same load, so
    nothing but the presence of B and C differs between the two experiments.
  </p>

  <h2>What the maps show</h2>
  <p>
    The page opens on the Coulomb excess
    <i>S</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;<i>&tau;<sub>s</sub></i>&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;<i>&mu;&sigma;<sub>n</sub>&prime;</i>
    of the three-fault system at a window of &plusmn;79&nbsp;MPa, the
    99th percentile of |<i>S</i>| over all elements. On a slipping element
    <i>S</i> is exactly zero &mdash; it is &minus;<i>w</i>, the
    solver&rsquo;s own margin &mdash; and those are 66&nbsp;%
    of the area, painted white. On a locked element it is the deficit below
    strength, painted blue: a median of 26&nbsp;MPa, and up to
    92 on the steepest facets of C, which under this load are the
    farthest from what it would take to move them. Switch the metric to the
    normal-stress change to see what the coupling would have fed back: slip
    clamps and unclamps the rough surfaces by &minus;10 to +12&nbsp;MPa on A, &minus;12 to +6 on B and &minus;6 to +4 on C&nbsp;MPa at a window
    of &plusmn;6.2; the shear change along the slip direction runs
    &minus;9 to +15&nbsp;MPa on A and &minus;9 to +13 on B&nbsp;MPa at a window of &plusmn;9.3. On a slipping
    element that shear is pinned by the friction law, so there the map is the
    remote deficit made up by slip; on a locked one it is what the neighbours
    transferred.
  </p>
  <p>
    Switch the experiment to <i>system&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;alone</i> and the map is what B and C do to A, at a window of its own: at the maps&rsquo; &plusmn;79&nbsp;MPa it would be invisible, and that is the first finding. It is a stress shadow, small and one-signed. The Coulomb excess on A falls by more than 0.5&nbsp;MPa over 8&nbsp;% of its area and by more than 1&nbsp;MPa over 2&nbsp;%, 2.6&nbsp;MPa at the most, in a patch 1 to 2&nbsp;km deep up-dip of B&rsquo;s trace; nowhere does it rise by more than 0.2. The normal stress on A is clamped by up to 1&nbsp;MPa beneath B and beneath C&rsquo;s root and unclamped by about as much elsewhere. The slip changes by millimetres in the median and 3&nbsp;cm at the most, and 19 of A&rsquo;s 3770 elements lock that slipped when A was alone (under the classical kernel 23 lock and one unlocks). That is the 2.4&nbsp;% of moment in the table, drawn where it happens.
  </p>

  <h2>Three kernels, one answer</h2>
  <p>
    The mollified kernel spreads each element&rsquo;s slip over a width
    <i>eps</i> on either side of its plane, which is what makes the stress
    on the element itself finite and interpretable; the classical element
    (cutde) is the exact dislocation with its edge singularities; the third
    kernel is the mollified one with <b>continuous quadratic slip</b>, one
    slip vector at every vertex and every edge midpoint shared by the
    elements that meet there (14,459 nodes), the condition enforced at
    the nodes and the rim of each fault held at zero, so that between the
    centroids the slip is a smooth surface rather than a staircase of line
    dislocations. On a frictionless penny crack that removes the residual
    between the centroids &mdash; a quarter of the stress drop in the facet
    cores for either P0 kernel, 0.2&nbsp;% for the continuous one &mdash;
    and brings the crack&rsquo;s moment to 1&nbsp;% of Eshelby&rsquo;s where
    one slip per element gives 7&nbsp;%.
  </p>
  <p>
    With the free surface folded into the kernel the three agree over the whole fault, trace rows included. The two P0 kernels, one slip per element, differ in their active sets on 3 of the 7058 elements (34 before the fold); on the locked elements their Coulomb excess differs by 0.03&nbsp;MPa in the median and 0.6 at the most, their normal-stress change by 0.04&nbsp;MPa in the median and 0.3 on the trace rows (6 to 8 there before), and their shear change by less than 0.01. The mollified moment is 2&nbsp;% above the classical on each fault, which is the mollification itself at <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i>; B&rsquo;s peak slip is 1.20&nbsp;m against 1.12. The continuous kernel runs at twice that <i>eps</i>: its moment is 6 to 9&nbsp;% above the classical, its trace-row normal-stress change within 1 to 2&nbsp;MPa of the classical one, and its active set differs on 97 elements. At its centroids the Coulomb excess of a slipping element is a readout <i>between</i> the nodes where the condition was enforced, not the solver&rsquo;s margin: 0.24&nbsp;% of strength in the median, 1.5&nbsp;% at the 90th percentile, 0.5&nbsp;% on the trace rows, and 17&nbsp;% on its worst element.
  </p>

  <h2>The free surface, folded</h2>
  <p>
    Until this revision the two mollified kernels disagreed with the
    classical one on exactly one row of elements: the ones that touch the
    surface. A mollified element spreads its slip over <i>eps</i> on either
    side of its plane, and for an element reaching <i>z</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0
    part of that smeared slip lies above ground. The kernel&rsquo;s image
    construction, Mindlin&rsquo;s, is right to second order in <i>eps</i>
    for a buried source, but continued above the surface it places its image
    inside the body, and within <i>eps</i> of the trace that is an
    O(1/<i>eps</i>) error. With one slip per element, sampled a third of an
    element below the surface, the error was <i>eps</i>&rsquo;s and the trace
    row slipped 40&nbsp;% too little; with slip continuous to the trace the
    nodes on the surface read it directly and slipped two to three times too
    much, moving the moment of the whole fault by 10 to 20&nbsp;%.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix is in the kernel and is the classical image idea applied to the
    smeared source: the part of the slip above the surface is folded back
    into the body as its mirror image, which is the same as adding a
    mirror-image fault with reflected slip. For a vertical strike-slip fault
    that alone is exact; for a dipping fault with dip slip the fault and its
    mirror meet at a kink along the trace, leave a normal traction on the
    free surface, and a Boussinesq correction removes it. Only the row of
    elements that touch the surface needs it, and only at points within one
    element height of the surface. Checked where the answer is known: on a
    vertical strike-slip fault against the antiplane solution the trace row
    went from 5.0&times; exact (continuous slip, collocation) and
    3.3&times; (Galerkin) to 0.98; on B alone, against the classical kernel,
    the continuous kernel&rsquo;s moment went from 0.82 of classical to
    1.01 with every depth bin within 3&nbsp;%. On this page it shows as the
    agreement of the three kernels on the trace rows: the normal-stress change on A&rsquo;s trace row reads &minus;4.5&nbsp;MPa in the median under the mollified P0 kernel against &minus;4.4 classical, and &minus;5.4 against &minus;5.1 on B, where before the fold it read +4.5 and +4.9 with maxima of +26 and +48; the continuous kernel reads &minus;3.6 and &minus;3.0 where it read +12.5 and +25.5 with a maximum of +143. The selfcheck pins the convergence on B alone: the classical-to-mollified slip ratio on the trace row is 0.94, 0.97, 0.995 at <i>eps</i>/<i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1, 0.05, 0.025 (1.60, 1.20, 1.05 before), and the readout of one fixed slip differs by 1.2&nbsp;MPa at 0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i> and then sits at a 0.2&nbsp;MPa floor, where it was 7.3&nbsp;MPa falling to 0.1 only at <i>eps</i>/<i>h</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.0125.
  </p>
  <p>
    What the continuous kernel still does not do is run at
    <i>eps</i>&nbsp;=&nbsp;0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i> in this, the collocation, form:
    its operator loses positive definiteness through broad body modes on
    each fault at that <i>eps</i> (fourteen broad modes on the three faults at 0.1&nbsp;<i>h</i>, down to &minus;13 against a median of +240, the asymmetry of collocation at a small <i>eps</i>/<i>h</i> on a large rough fault), so it is solved at
    0.2&nbsp;<i>h</i> by an active-set Newton method, where it converges in
    six iterations (its symmetric part on the free nodes has a smallest eigenvalue of &minus;1.6 against a median of about +200, a long-wavelength mode of the kind the two P0 operators carry on this system as well). The symmetric Galerkin form
    of the same kernel is positive definite at every <i>eps</i> and, with the
    fold, matches the classical solve on B to 1&nbsp;%; it costs four times
    the assembly and is the form to build the next page on.
  </p>

  <h2>Signs and windows</h2>
  <p>
    Traction is <i>&sigma;</i>&middot;<i>n</i> with each facet&rsquo;s upward
    normal, so its normal component is tension positive; the normal stress in
    the Coulomb strength is compression positive, which is the sign the
    friction module uses, and the two are pinned against each other rather
    than reasoned about. Every window is symmetric, &plusmn;<i>W</i>, with
    <i>W</i> the area-weighted 99th percentile of the P0 <i>mollified</i> map
    of the three-fault system rounded to two figures, and it is the same
    window for every kernel and both experiments; the slider moves it and the
    legend counts what saturates. Nothing is smoothed, decimated or
    interpolated: each element carries the sample taken at its centroid,
    0.29&nbsp;<i>h</i> from its edges on an equilateral element and never
    nearer than 1.3&nbsp;<i>eps</i> on this mesh.
  </p>
  <p class="cap">
    Every array ships with its byte count and sha256 in
    <a href={`${base}data/onfault_manifest.json`}>onfault_manifest.json</a>,
    with the geometry of the junction, the load, the ladder it was chosen from,
    the window rule, both experiments&rsquo; active fractions, moments and
    peak slips per kernel, and every kernel&rsquo;s definiteness, sweep
    counts and residuals. See <a href={`${base}roughness/`}>Rough crack</a>
    for the kernel head-to-head at the centroids of a buried crack,
    <a href={`${base}isosurface/`}>Isosurfaces</a> for the same disagreement
    off the fault, and <a href={`${base}concepts/`}>Concepts</a> for what
    <i>eps</i> is.
  </p>

  <script>
    import { startOnFault } from "../viz/onfault";
    startOnFault(document.getElementById("viewer")!, import.meta.env.BASE_URL);
  </script>
</Base>
